  "summary": "I reconciled all three patients per RBC-TOC-02. Each has a progress note, plus an addendum note because new results and documents arrived while I was working (§8) and changed decisions for all three.\n\nWALTER KIM (pt-0005)\n- Furosemide: changed from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily (new order mr-t0001). The signed addendum (doc-00009) overrides the 40 mg daily in the original summary.\n- Potassium chloride: stopped (mr-00076), as the discharge summary says.\n- Pantoprazole: not ordered. It was an inpatient formulary substitution, so his home omeprazole continues.\n- Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin and aspirin: continued unchanged.\n- Lisinopril: I first kept it on hold because potassium was 5.4 on 10/21, and set you a task (task-t0003). A repeat BMP came in this morning (10/23): K 4.7, SCr 1.21. That meets the discharge condition, so I resumed lisinopril 10 mg daily (mr-00072).\n- For you:\n  - task-t0003 is no longer needed, but the system wouldn't let me cancel it. Please close it (explained in comm-t0015).\n  - An unsigned draft St. Brigid addendum (doc-00902) proposes furosemide 60 mg twice daily. I didn't act on it because only signed documents count. You may want to confirm the dose with St. Brigid.\n\nTERENCE STARK (pt-0004)\n- Carvedilol: I first raised it to 12.5 mg twice daily, as the summary listed (as Coreg). A signed addendum arrived today (doc-00904): the increase was reversed before discharge for a heart rate of 48–52 and lightheadedness on standing. I put him back on 6.25 mg twice daily (mr-t0012).\n- Insulin 70/30 18 units twice daily and aspirin: unchanged.\n- Flagged to you:\n  - **Atorvastatin 80 mg** (comm-t0006): the discharge summary continues it, but his last fill was 03/04, covering only about 24% of the 180 days before admission. I made no change.\n  - **Glipizide 5 mg** (comm-t0007): filled monthly at an outside pharmacy, prescribed by Dr. Desai (Valley Endocrinology). The discharge summary doesn't mention it and we have no order for it, so I didn't order or change it. He is also on insulin 70/30, which together raise the risk of low blood sugar.\n\nEARNEST ROBERTS (pt-0044)\n- Lisinopril: I resumed it based on the 10/22 BMP (SCr 1.08, K 4.6), which met the discharge condition (SCr below 1.3). A new potassium of 5.5 then came in this morning (10/23), so I put it back on hold (mr-00373). I set you a task, due 10/27, to repeat potassium and decide on resuming (task-t0014).\n- Insulin glargine, insulin lispro and rosuvastatin: unchanged.\n\nI closed the four inbox items for the arrivals. The rest of your inbox is for other patients and I left it untouched.",
